<<<
partially is often used where partly would be better.
===
**partially** is often used where *partly* would be better.
>>>

<<<
because it is formed normally, by way of the adjective partial, while partly formed direct from the noun part is abnormal.
===
because it is formed normally, by way of the adjective *partial*, while *partly* formed direct from the noun *part* is abnormal.
>>>

<<<
as between wholly (opp. partly) & completely (opp. partially); in other words, partly is better
===
as between *wholly* (opp. *partly*) & *completely* (opp. *partially*); in other words, *partly* is better
>>>

<<<
& not the whole ’, & partially in the sense ‘ to a limited degree’: It is partly wood; This was partly due to cowardice; | A partially drunken sailor; His partially re-esiablished health.
===
& not the whole ’, & *partially* in the sense ‘ to a limited degree’: *It is partly wood; This was partly due to cowardice; A partially drunken sailor; His partially re-established health.*
>>>

<<<
well; partly is then recom- | mended, since it is partially that tends to be over-used; see LONG VARIANTS for other such pairs. An | example or two of the wrong partially are:—
===
well; *partly* is then recommended, since it is *partially* that tends to be over-used; see ^^LONG VARIANTS^^ for other such pairs. An example or two of the wrong *partially* are:—
>>>

<<<
The two fect are partially of iron & partially of clay./Whether The Case is Altered may be wholly or partially or not at all assignable to the hand of Jonson.
===
*The two feet are partially of iron & partially of clay./Whether* The Case is Altered *may be wholly or partially or not at all assignable to the hand of Jonson.*
>>>

